=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [skp-beliefs-118] AI Creates Economically Productive but Socially Harmful Outputs: Ecological fallacy / attribution error — Societal harms from addictive platforms or manipulative ads are attributed to 'AI outputs' specifically, when the same harms substantially predate AI systems and stem from underlying business model incentives. Conflating the platform incentive problem with an AI-specific problem weakens the causal chain.
- [skp-beliefs-254] Human Fracking: Weak Analogy — The comparison between human attention and environmental resources like oil (fracking) might overlook significant disanalogies, potentially leading to misleading conclusions about the nature of the harm or the appropriate remedies.
- [skp-beliefs-045] AI-Generated Content Causes Direct, Documented Harm to Individuals: Instrument/Agent Conflation — The claim frames AI as a direct causal agent of harm, when the proximate cause is malicious human intent; AI lowers friction but is not itself the harm-producing force, which risks misdirecting regulatory responses toward the tool rather than perpetrators.
- [skp-beliefs-101] AI Investment Is Built on Unsustainable Economics and Overstated Capabilities: Conflation — The node conflates two distinct claims — unsustainable economics (a structural/financial argument) and overstated capabilities (an epistemic/fraud argument) — treating them as a unified phenomenon when they have different causes, evidence bases, and policy remedies.
- [skp-beliefs-141] AI Personhood as a Corporate Liability Shield: Slippery Slope — The node assumes that any movement toward AI legal personhood will inevitably be exploited for liability evasion, without adequately accounting for countervailing legal safeguards, judicial interpretation, or regulatory design that could prevent such misuse.
- [skp-beliefs-057] Public Resistance to AI Reflects Accumulated Distrust from Prior Tech Cycles: Post hoc ergo propter hoc — The claim assumes that because prior tech cycles preceded current AI distrust, those cycles caused it—but contemporary AI skepticism may stem primarily from AI-specific concerns (job displacement, deepfakes, LLM hallucinations) rather than inherited grievances.
- [skp-intentions-008] Replace Architectural Pre-Clearance with Purpose-Bound Data Licenses: False Dichotomy — The node frames the choice as binary between 'architectural pre-clearance' and 'purpose-bound data licenses,' obscuring the possibility that both mechanisms, or hybrid approaches, might be necessary for comprehensive oversight.
- [skp-beliefs-170] AI Corporations Systematically Evade Effective Regulation: Hasty Generalization — Drawing a 'systematic' pattern of evasion from a set of documented but selective cases (audit washing, compute thresholds, acquihires) risks overgeneralizing to the entire AI corporate sector without accounting for firms with substantive compliance efforts.

